canonicalMap -- gets the natural map arising from various constructions
***********************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        g = canonicalMap(D, C)
  * Inputs:
      * C, a *note complex: Complex,, 
      * D, a *note complex: Complex,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * UseTarget => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default
        value null, determines the choice of canonical map when $D$ is a
        cylinder of a map $f$ and the source and target of $f$ are the same
  * Outputs:
      * g, a *note map of complexes: ComplexMap,, 

Description
===========

A canonical map, also called a natural map, is a map that arises naturally from
the definition or the construction of the object.

The following six constructions are supported: kernel, cokernel, image,
coimage, cone, and cylinder.

The *note kernel of a complex map: kernel_lpComplexMap_rp, comes with a natural
injection into the source complex. This natural map is always a complex
morphism.

