complex(Module) -- make a chain complex of length zero
******************************************************

  * Function: *note complex: complex_lpList_rp,
  * Usage: 
        complex M
  * Inputs:
      * M, a *note module: (Macaulay2Doc)Module,, or *note Ideal:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, or *note Ring: (Macaulay2Doc)Ring,.
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Base (missing documentation) => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,,
        default value 0, index for M
  * Outputs:
      * a *note complex: Complex,, returns the complex whose 0-th component is
        M.

Description
===========

In contrast to *note complex(HashTable): complex_lpHashTable_rp, and *note
complex(List): complex_lpList_rp,, this constructor provides a convenient
method to construct a complex with only one non-zero component.

We illustrate this with a free module.

+-----------------------+
|i1 : S = ZZ/101[a..d]  |
|                       |
|o1 = S                 |
|                       |
|o1 : PolynomialRing    |
+-----------------------+
|i2 : C0 = complex S^2  |
|                       |
|      2                |
|o2 = S                 |
|                       |
|     0                 |
|                       |
|o2 : Complex           |
+-----------------------+
|i3 : f = dd^C0         |
|                       |
|o3 = 0                 |
|                       |
|o3 : ComplexMap        |
+-----------------------+
|i4 : source f, target f|
|                       |
|       2   2           |
|o4 = (S , S )          |
|                       |
|      0   0            |
|                       |
|o4 : Sequence          |
+-----------------------+
|i5 : f == 0            |
|                       |
|o5 = true              |
+-----------------------+
|i6 : isWellDefined C0  |
|                       |
|o6 = true              |
+-----------------------+
|i7 : C0 == 0           |
|                       |
|o7 = false             |
+-----------------------+
|i8 : length C0         |
|                       |
|o8 = 0                 |
+-----------------------+
+-------------------------------+
|i9 : C1 = complex(S^2, Base=>3)|
|                               |
|      2                        |
|o9 = S                         |
|                               |
|     3                         |
|                               |
|o9 : Complex                   |
+-------------------------------+
|i10 : C1 == C0[-3]             |
|                               |
|o10 = true                     |
+-------------------------------+
|i11 : C1_3                     |
|                               |
|       2                       |
|o11 = S                        |
|                               |
|o11 : S-module, free           |
+-------------------------------+
|i12 : C1_0                     |
|                               |
|o12 = 0                        |
|                               |
|o12 : S-module                 |
+-------------------------------+

A ring or an ideal will be converted to a module first.

+---------------------------+
|i13 : C2 = complex S       |
|                           |
|       1                   |
|o13 = S                    |
|                           |
|      0                    |
|                           |
|o13 : Complex              |
+---------------------------+
|i14 : I = ideal(a^2-b, c^3)|
|                           |
|              2       3    |
|o14 = ideal (a  - b, c )   |
|                           |
|o14 : Ideal of S           |
+---------------------------+
|i15 : C3 = complex I       |
|                           |
|o15 = image | a2-b c3 |    |
|                           |
|      0                    |
|                           |
|o15 : Complex              |
+---------------------------+
|i16 : C4 = complex (S/I)   |
|                           |
|      /      S     \1      |
|o16 = |------------|       |
|      |  2       3 |       |
|      \(a  - b, c )/       |
|                           |
|      0                    |
|                           |
|o16 : Complex              |
+---------------------------+
|i17 : (ring C3, ring C4)   |
|                           |
|                S          |
|o17 = (S, ------------)    |
|            2       3      |
|          (a  - b, c )     |
|                           |
|o17 : Sequence             |
+---------------------------+

The zero complex over a ring S is most conveniently created by giving the zero
module.

+----------------------+
|i18 : C5 = complex S^0|
|                      |
|o18 = 0               |
|                      |
|      0               |
|                      |
|o18 : Complex         |
+----------------------+
|i19 : C5 == 0         |
|                      |
|o19 = true            |
+----------------------+
|i20 : dd^C5 == 0      |
|                      |
|o20 = true            |
+----------------------+
|i21 : C5_0            |
|                      |
|o21 = 0               |
|                      |
|o21 : S-module        |
+----------------------+

See also
========

  * *note Making chain complexes: Making chain complexes, -- information about
    the basic constructors
  * *note isWellDefined(Complex): isWellDefined_lpComplex_rp, -- whether a
    complex is well-defined
  * *note complex(HashTable): complex_lpHashTable_rp, -- make a chain complex

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * "complex(Ideal)"
  * *note complex(Module): complex_lpModule_rp, -- make a chain complex of
    length zero
  * "complex(Ring)"
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

