extend(Complex,Complex,Matrix,Sequence) -- extend a map of modules to a map of chain complexes
**********************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note extend: (Macaulay2Doc)extend,
  * Usage: 
        g = extend(D, C, f, p)
        g = extend(D, C, f)
  * Inputs:
      * C, a *note complex: Complex,, 
      * D, a *note complex: Complex,, 
      * f, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, 
      * p, a *note sequence: (Macaulay2Doc)Sequence,, consisting of a pair of
        integers $(j,i)$, such that the matrix $f$ defines a map from $C_i$ to
        $D_j$; the default value is $i = j = 0$
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * Verify (missing documentation) => a *note Boolean value:
        (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value true, currently, this option is
        ignored
  * Outputs:
      * a *note map of complexes: ComplexMap,, 

Description
===========

Let $C$ be a chain complex such that each term is a free module.  Let $D$ be a
chain complex which is exact at the $k$-th term for all $k > j$. Given a map of
modules $f \colon C_i \to D_j$ such that the image of $f \circ
\operatorname{dd}^C_{i+1}$ is contained in the image of
$\operatorname{dd}^D_{j+1}$, this method constructs a morphism of chain
complexes $g \colon C \to D$ of degree $j-i$ such that $g_i = f$.

$\phantom{WWWW} \begin{array}{cccccc} 0 & \!\!\leftarrow\!\! & C_{i} &
\!\!\leftarrow\!\! & C_{i+1} & \!\!\leftarrow\!\! & C_{i+2} & \dotsb \\ &
& \downarrow \, {\scriptstyle f} & & \downarrow \, {\scriptstyle g_{i+1}} &&
\downarrow \, {\scriptstyle g_{i+2}} \\ 0 & \!\!\leftarrow\!\! & D_{j} &
\!\!\leftarrow\!\! & D_{j+1} &  \!\!\leftarrow\!\! & D_{j+2} & \dotsb \\
\end{array} $

A map between modules extends to a map between their free resolutions.

+---------------------------------------------+
|i1 : S = ZZ/101[a..d];                       |
+---------------------------------------------+
|i2 : I = ideal(a*b*c, b*c*d, a*d^2)          |
|                                             |
|                             2               |
|o2 = ideal (a*b*c, b*c*d, a*d )              |
