There is a simple relationship between Hom complexes and *note shifts: Complex
Array,. Specifically, shifting the first argument is the same as the negative
shift of the result.  But shifting the second argument is only the same as the
positive shift of the result up to a sign.

+--------------------------------------------+
|i9 : Hom(C[3], C) == D[-3]                  |
|                                            |
|o9 = true                                   |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i10 : Hom(C, C[-2]) == D[-2]                |
|                                            |
|o10 = true                                  |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i11 : Hom(C, C[-3]) != D[-3]                |
|                                            |
|o11 = true                                  |
+--------------------------------------------+
|i12 : Hom(C, C[-3]) == complex(- dd^(D[-3]))|
|                                            |
|o12 = true                                  |
+--------------------------------------------+

Specific maps and morphisms between complexes can be obtained with *note
homomorphism(ComplexMap): homomorphism_lpComplexMap_rp,.

Because the Hom complex can be regarded as the total complex of a double
complex, each term comes with pairs of indices, labelling the summands.

