inducedMap(Complex,Complex) -- make the map of complexes induced at each term by the identity map
*************************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note inducedMap: (Macaulay2Doc)inducedMap,
  * Usage: 
        f = inducedMap(D, C)
  * Inputs:
      * C, a *note complex: Complex,, 
      * D, a *note complex: Complex,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degree: (Macaulay2Doc)inducedMap_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmDegree=>_pd_pd_pd_
        rp, => an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, default value null, specify
        the degree of the map of complexes, if not 0
      * *note Verify: (Macaulay2Doc)inducedMap_lp_pd_pd_pd_cmVerify=>_pd_pd_pd_
        rp, => a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, default value
        true, if true, check that the resulting maps are well-defined
  * Outputs:
      * f, a *note map of complexes: ComplexMap,, 

Description
===========

Let $d$ be the value of the optional argument Degree, or zero, if not given.
For each $i$, the terms $D_{i+d}$ and $C_i$ must be subquotients of the same
ambient free module.  This method returns the complex map induced by the
identity on each of these free modules.

If Verify => true is given, then this method also checks that these identity
maps induced well-defined maps.  This can be a relatively expensive
computation.

We illustrate this method by truncating a free resolution at two distinct
internal degrees.  We check that the various induced maps compose to give
another induced map.

