naiveTruncation(ComplexMap,ZZ,ZZ) -- drops all terms in the source of a complex outside a given interval
********************************************************************************************************

  * Function: *note naiveTruncation: naiveTruncation_lpComplex_cmZZ_cmZZ_rp,
  * Usage: 
        naiveTruncation(f, lo, hi)
        naiveTruncation(f, (lo, hi))
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note map of complexes: ComplexMap,, 
      * lo, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, or -infinity or null (the
        latter two give no lower bound)
      * hi, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, or infinity or null (the
        latter two give no upper bound)
  * Outputs:
      * a *note map of complexes: ComplexMap,, 

Description
===========

Naive truncations of complexes are functorial.  Given a map of chain complexes,
this method returns the canonical map from the naive truncation of the source
to the naive truncation of the target.  The degree of f is used to determine
the appropriate naive truncation of the target complex.

First, we define some non-trivial maps of chain complexes.

+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/101[a..d];                                                     |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : C = (freeResolution coker matrix{{a,b,c}})[1]                         |
|                                                                           |
|      1      3      3      1                                               |
|o2 = R  <-- R  <-- R  <-- R                                                |
|                                                                           |
|     -1     0      1      2                                                |
|                                                                           |
|o2 : Complex                                                               |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : D = freeResolution coker matrix{{a*b,a*c,b*c}}                        |
|                                                                           |
|      1      3      2                                                      |
|o3 = R  <-- R  <-- R                                                       |
|                                                                           |
|     0      1      2                                                       |
|                                                                           |
|o3 : Complex                                                               |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : E = freeResolution coker matrix{{a^2,b^2,c*d}}                        |
|                                                                           |
|      1      3      3      1                                               |
|o4 = R  <-- R  <-- R  <-- R                                                |
|                                                                           |
|     0      1      2      3                                                |
|                                                                           |
|o4 : Complex                                                               |
+---------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : f = randomComplexMap(D, C)                                            |
|                                                                           |
